    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2NidJm7Tzd128tJp4sbpQtK)_\n\nFound while looking for the Emacs start-up hang. In a batch profile of a copy of the real vault and `vulpea.db`, 56% of the CPU over 145 s was `vulpea-select-cache-prewarm-step`, which reads every row of the database into a candidate cache for `vulpea-find` and `vulpea-insert` as soon as `vulpea-db-autosync-mode` starts (500-note chunks on the main thread; `vulpea-select.el` lines 547 and 1036 to 1092).\n\n- `(setq vulpea-select-cache-prewarm nil)` in the vulpea base settings. The cache is then built at the first `vulpea-find` or `vulpea-insert`, which pauses once.\n- New option `sp-vulpea-autosync-delay` (30 idle seconds, was a fixed 5) for starting autosync.\n\nOrg doc edited first, `.el` to match; CHANGELOG entry added. Not run in Emacs (none in the container). The hang is not confirmed fixed: a native sample of the live Emacs also showed `org-element` parsing from the autosync indexing itself.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiBTiUziZidcdSerB8r)_",
